description:
## What
`aggregate/scorecard.mjs` inlines report-referenced screenshots by joining a model-written path to the run directory with no containment check. A `report.artifacts[].path` such as `../../../../home/user/.ssh/id_rsa.png` resolves outside the run directory, `existsSync` confirms it, and `readFileSync` inlines the bytes as a base64 data URI into `scorecard.html`.

## Evidence (verified in code at origin/master 2649765)
- `aggregate/scorecard.mjs:186-195` — the whole helper:
  ```js
  function dataUri(dir, relPath) {
    try {
      const abs = join(dir, relPath);
      if (!existsSync(abs)) return null;
      const ext = relPath.split('.').pop().toLowerCase();
      const mime = { png: ..., jpg: ..., jpeg: ..., webp: ..., gif: ... }[ext];
      if (!mime) return null;
      const b64 = readFileSync(abs, { encoding: 'base64' });
      return `data:${mime};base64,${b64}`;
  ```
  `join` normalises `..` segments; there is no `resolve`/`startsWith` containment test and no `..`-segment rejection.
- `aggregate/scorecard.mjs:284-289` — the tainted input reaches it directly: `const rel = \`${encodeURI(report.__runId)}/${a.path}\`; ... const inline = dataUri(dir, a.path) ?? rel;` where `a.path` comes from `report.artifacts`.
- `report.json` is model-written (the agent writes it; `fixer/fix.mjs` explicitly treats "a report file is untrusted input" at its `countOutstanding` guard) and its contents are influenced by untrusted page content.
- Effect is bounded by the image-extension filter: this is an arbitrary *image* read plus a file-existence oracle, and the bytes render into the published scorecard. It is not arbitrary-file/text read.

## Why it is real
This is a stated security invariant, not a style preference.
`~/agents/findings/web-uplift-factory-THREAT_MODEL.md` section 6, **I6**: "Every report string rendered into `scorecard.html` must be HTML-escaped; **screenshots referenced by path must stay inside the run directory**."
Section 5, HS5 asks exactly this verification question ("can a crafted URL or host produce a path escaping the report root?").
NOTE: the vuln-triage finding text cites invariant **I9**; that is a miscitation (I9 is the vendored-copy regeneration rule). The correct invariant is I6. Recorded here so the bead is self-consistent.

## Calibrated severity
Station severity: medium (vuln-triage). Conditional on an attacker-influenced `report.json`, and the read is image-extension-limited, so P3 rather than P1. The fix is small and local.

## Station citation
Factory audit run 2026-10-06T00:22Z, target `/home/exedev/worktrees/web-uplift-factory` @4cccc60, vuln-triage finding `vuln-triage/path-traversal-scorecard-artifact-read` (report `/home/exedev/agents/runs/vuln-triage-web-uplift-factory-20261006-003424/report.json`). Hand-calibrated because the findings store records every finding as CRITICAL (fleet-eqv).

## Bounded next step
Inside `dataUri`, resolve first and reject anything not contained:
```js
const base = resolve(dir);
const abs = resolve(dir, relPath);
if (abs !== base && !abs.startsWith(base + sep)) return null;
```
Also reject absolute paths and any `..` segment, and add a regression case with a `../` path to the scorecard tests.

